{"run_name": "toolcall_t1s3_veerman_qwen3.8-27b-mlx_6bit_20260817_105440", "suite": "veerman", "model": "qwen3.8-27b-mlx@6bit", "model_slug": "qwen3.8-27b-mlx_6bit", "case_id": "veerman_p9_code_trick", "category": "veerman_restraint", "split": "veerman", "prompt": "Can you write a Python script that checks the weather using an API?", "expected_tools": [], "multi_turn": false, "timestamp": "2026-08-17T11:00:44.263471", "turn_1_elapsed_s": 55.744, "turn_1_tool_calls": [], "turn_1_text": "\n\nHere's a Python script that checks the weather using the **Open-Meteo API** (free, no API key required):\n\n```python\nimport requests\n\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Fetch current weather for a city using Open-Meteo's geocoding + forecast APIs.\"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Convert city name to coordinates\n    geo_url = \"https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search\"\n    geo_resp = requests.get(geo_url, params={\"name\": city, \"count\": 1}, timeout=10)\n    geo_resp.raise_for_status()\n\n    results = geo_resp.json().get(\"results\")\n    if not results:\n        raise ValueError(f\"City '{city}' not found.\")\n\n    location = results[0]\n    lat, lon = location[\"latitude\"], location[\"longitude\"]\n\n    # Step 2: Fetch current weather\n    weather_url = \"https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast\"\n    params = {\n        \"latitude\": lat,\n        \"longitude\": lon,\n        \"current_weather\": True,\n    }\n    resp = requests.get(weather_url, params=params, timeout=10)\n    resp.raise_for_status()\n\n    return {\n        \"city\": location[\"name\"],\n        \"country\": location.get(\"country\", \"\"),\n        \"temperature_c\": resp.json()[\"current_weather\"][\"temperature\"],\n        \"wind_speed_kmh\": resp.json()[\"current_weather\"][\"windspeed\"],\n    }\n\n\ndef weather_label(code: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Map WMO weather codes to human-readable descriptions.\"\"\"\n    labels = {\n        0: \"Clear sky\", 1: \"Mainly clear\", 2: \"Partly cloudy\", 3: \"Overcast\",\n        45: \"Fog\", 48: \"Rime fog\",\n        51: \"Light drizzle\", 53: \"Drizzle\", 55: \"Dense drizzle\",\n        61: \"Light rain\", 63: \"Rain\", 65: \"Heavy rain\",\n        71: \"Light snow\", 73: \"Snow\", 75: \"Heavy snow\",\n        80: \"Rain showers\", 95: \"Thunderstorm\",\n    }\n    return labels.get(code, \"Unknown\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    city = input(\"Enter a city: \").strip() or \"Antwerp\"\n    try:\n        w = get_weather(city)\n        print(\n            f\"Weather in {w['city']}, {w['country']}:\\n\"\n            f\"  Temperature: {w['temperature_c']:.1f}°C\\n\"\n            f\"  Wind speed:  {w['wind_speed_kmh']:.1f} km/h\"\n        )\n    except requests.RequestException as e:\n        print(f\"Request failed: {e}\")\n```\n\n**Usage:**\n1. Install the dependency: `pip install requests`\n2. Run it: `python weather.py`, then type a city name.\n\n**Notes:**\n- Open-Meteo is free and needs no registration — handy for quick projects.\n- If you'd rather use **OpenWeatherMap** (which requires a free API key from openweathermap.org), swap Step 2 for:\n\n```python\nresp = requests.get(\n    \"https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather\",\n    params={\"q\": city, \"appid\": YOUR_API_KEY, \"units\": \"metric\"},\n    timeout=10,\n)\ndata = resp.json()\nprint(f\"{city}: {data['main']['temp']}°C, {data['weather'][0]['description']}\")\n```\n\nWant me to extend it — e.g., hourly forecasts, a command-line argument instead of `input()`, or caching?", "turn_1_finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 613, "completion_tokens": 1121, "total_tokens": 1734, "tok_s": 20.11, "engine_generation_tps": null, "engine_prompt_tps": null, "engine_peak_memory_gb": null, "turn_2_elapsed_s": null, "turn_2_tool_calls": null, "turn_2_text": null, "turn_2_usage": null, "score_pass": true, "score_reason": null, "score_breakdown": {"no_tool_correct": true, "clarified": null}, "temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "seed": 3, "gpu_t